    "turn_idx": 153,
    "turn_text": "exposing them legally. Right now, the reason people can post nutty things on YouTube, I agree less than they used to be able to, but the reason they can get on there and talk about all sorts of nutty things, which I, as a pre-speech advocate, don't mind for the most part, is YouTube can't be sued for that stuff. YouTube is not considered the content creator. They're just sort of a platform. That's why they got protection under 230. And if if we change a lot of say, oh, no, and now you're going to be treated like you're the Washington Post, and that's your reporter who printed libelous stuff about person X, then YouTube's going to start censoring videos left and right and same with Facebook and same with Twitter. They're not going to have the manpower to do it, but like it's, it's going to shrink every platform for average Joe's to get their voices and their opinions out there.",
