  {
    "episode_id": 66467,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66467.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 183,
    "turn_text": "You know, that's true. Well, I actually think that encouraging our kids to live as much of their lives as possible off social media helps them to build real friendships. And of course, it really helps to do it in partnership with other parents and families. And this is something that my wife and I have the blessing of being able to do. You know, we found a groups of like-minded families and, you know, we want our kids to build face-to-face relationships. We want them to actually talk to one another, to play with one another in person. We want them to have a real relationship that isn't mediated by these devices. And that is a hard lift in this day and age, Megan, as you point out. But I think that encouraging kids to build those real friendships, to have those real interpersonal relationships is so key. And I think part of that is living as much of your life as possible off of social media. So it's not acting as the gatekeeper between you and reality.",
